From 5f10d6257ef0c8c8b69b18a2a23ec351f6e68be2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: EcoWoods Build <build@ecowoods.ca>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 23:22:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(ui): three legibility defects on commercial pages, and
 the guard that finds them
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Found by looking at a screenshot. All three shipped green through thirty-eight
guards, because every one of them checks what the page SAYS and none checked
whether a human can read it. All three are on the highest-intent pages.

F-153 — TEXT PAINTED OVER TEXT ON /hardwood-stairs-toronto

IllustrationPair rendered its caption inside `.ilpair-layer`, which is
`position: absolute; inset: 0` on a stage whose height comes from the image
ratio and nothing else. The caption had no height budget, spilled out of flow,
and the next section — the grey spec list — was painted straight over it. Two
paragraphs of live copy overlapping, plus the "View full size" link, unreadable.

The component's own premise is that these are one fact seen twice. One fact
takes ONE caption, in normal flow, below the stage. `Illustration` gains
`caption={false}` for callers that render it themselves. The full-size link now
points at the drawing currently showing — and at the BUNDLED url, not
`public/`, which does not serve on this deployment (F-131 again).

Affects every <IllustrationPair> on the site, twenty of them on the stairs page
alone.

F-154 — THE GREY SLAB

`.gd-spec` paints `--line` and depends on each child painting `--surface-1` back
over it, with the 1px grid gap left showing as the divider. Eight blocks had
children with no `.gd-spec-row`, so the whole list rendered as an unpadded grey
slab, labels at 2.29:1 instead of 5.88:1, the two-column layout never applying.

Among them: the "Related questions this guide answers" block on ALL SIXTEEN
GUIDES, and four of the five commercial pages.

Fixed in two places on purpose. Fourteen rows get the class. And `.gd-spec > *`
now paints its own ground whatever it is — a row div, or the bare `<dt>/<dd>`
pairs /press uses, which are the more correct HTML of the two. The broken state
is now "no divider lines", not "unreadable". A component whose failure mode is
invisible text will fail invisibly again.

F-155 — NINETY-SIX INVISIBLE LINKS

`.footer-links` was reused verbatim on /hardwood-flooring-toronto,
/hardwood-floor-refinishing-toronto and /hardwood-stairs-toronto. Its colour is
rgba(245,239,230,0.7): correct on the dark footer, **1.04:1** on the cream page
background. Thirty-two service-area links on each of the three commercial pages,
invisible to a visitor. Legible on hover at 2.27:1, which still fails AA.

That is not only an accessibility failure. Text a crawler reads and a visitor
cannot see is the shape of hidden text, and these are the pages carrying the
money queries. New `.area-links`: `--ink-soft` at 14.24:1, `--copper-text` at
4.81:1 on hover, the 44px tap target from F-31 kept.

THE GUARD, AND WHY IT IS A SHORT LIST RATHER THAN A CLEVER ALGORITHM

The first version inferred the rule — light text with no ground of its own must
sit under a dark ancestor, ancestry walked from the JSX. It reported 66
violations of which 3 were real. Every false one was a dark ground arriving
from somewhere a flat CSS parser cannot follow: a gradient on a parent, a
`--dark` modifier applied by a sibling, a card whose ground is a photograph.

A guard that is wrong 95% of the time does not get fixed. It gets ignored, and
then it is worse than nothing because it looks like coverage.

So scripts/verify-ui-contract.mjs FAILS on three things it can prove: a
`footer-*` class outside the footer, a `.gd-spec` row without `.gd-spec-row`,
and the removal of the `.gd-spec > *` fail-safe. The general contrast scan still
runs, as a ranked ADVISORY with real numbers — the same shape as the unsourced-
claim queue. Anything in it that turns out to be real gets promoted into
CONTEXT_RULES, where it becomes a hard failure forever.

One advisory entry deserves a decision rather than a shrug, and is deliberately
not changed here: `--copper` (#c87e4f) is 2.97:1 on cream, and it is the colour
of every breadcrumb, every "Read more", every `.clp-more`. Legible, below the
4.5:1 AA asks for normal text. `--copper-text` (#9f5c32) is 4.81:1 and is
already in the palette. That is a brand call, not a bug.

TESTED — each hard check re-broken and confirmed to fire, then restored:
removing the fail-safe, dropping one `.gd-spec-row`, putting `.footer-links`
back on a commercial page. 39 guards green.

